To make Bootstrap tables sortable alphanumerically by clicking the headers without using jQuery or other plugins, you can use plain JavaScript. Here's a simple example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Sortable Table</title>
  <link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.0-alpha1/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet" integrity="sha384-KyZXEAg3QhqLMpG8r+Knujsl7/1L_dstPt3HV70md2tm1DtDC+px+jBdUJQD8kY4" crossorigin="anonymous">
</head>
<body>
  <table class="table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th onclick="sortTable(0)">Name</th>
        <th onclick="sortTable(1)">Age</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody id="myTable">
      <tr>
        <td>John Doe</td>
        <td>30</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Jane Smith</td>
        <td>28</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Mike Johnson</td>
        <td>35</td>
      </tr>
      <!-- Add more rows as needed -->
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <script>
    function sortTable(n) {
      var table, rows, switching, i, x, y, shouldSwitch, dir, switchcount = 0;
      table = document.getElementById("myTable");
      switching = true;
      dir = "asc";
      while (switching) {
        switching = false;
        rows = table.rows;
        for (i = 1; i < (rows.length - 1); i++) {
          shouldSwitch = false;
          x = rows[i].getElementsByTagName("TD")[n];
          y = rows[i + 1].getElementsByTagName("TD")[n];
          if (dir == "asc") {
            if (x.innerHTML.toLowerCase() > y.innerHTML.toLowerCase()) {
              shouldSwitch = true;
              break;
            }
          } else if (dir == "desc") {
            if (x.innerHTML.toLowerCase() < y.innerHTML.toLowerCase()) {
              shouldSwitch = true;
              break;
            }
          }
        }
        if (shouldSwitch) {
          rows[i].parentNode.insertBefore(rows[i + 1], rows[i]);
          switching = true;
          switchcount ++;
        }
      }
      if (switchcount == 0 && dir == "asc") {
        dir = "desc";
        sortTable(n);
      }
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This example creates a simple table with two columns: Name and Age. When you click on the headers, the table rows will be sorted alphanumerically. The sorting function `sortTable()` takes an argument `n` representing the column index to sort by. The sorting algorithm used is a simple bubble sort, which is not the most efficient for large datasets, but it works well for small tables.

You can customize the table structure, column names, and sorting logic according to your needs.

I'm sorry for any confusion. To help you better, I'll need to see the specific diagram you're referring to. However, I can provide general guidance on how to create a flowchart in Graphviz, which is a popular tool for creating diagrams.

1. Install Graphviz: You can download it from https://graphviz.org/download/.

2. Create a .dot file: This is the file where you'll write your flowchart. Here's a simple example:

```
digraph G {
    A -> B [label="Operation 1"];
    B -> C [label="Operation 2"];
    C -> D [label="Operation 3"];
}
```

In this example, `A`, `B`, `C`, and `D` are nodes, and the arrows represent the flow. The `[label="Operation 1"]` part is the label for the arrow.

3. Add text to nodes: You can add text to nodes like this:

```
digraph G {
    A [label="Start"];
    B [label="Operation 1"];
    C [label="Operation 2"];
    D [label="End"];
    A -> B;
    B -> C;
    C -> D;
}
```

4. Shape of nodes: You can change the shape of nodes using the `shape` attribute. For example, to make a node a rectangle:

```
A [shape=rectangle, label="Start"];
```

5. Render the .dot file: You can render the .dot file using the `dot` command in the terminal:

```
dot -Tpng flowchart.dot -o flowchart.png
```

This will create a PNG image of your flowchart named `flowchart.png`. You can replace `flowchart.dot` with the name of your .dot file and `flowchart.png` with the name you want for the output image.

Remember to adjust the size of the nodes and the positions of the arrows using the `size` and `pos` attributes, respectively. For example:

```
A [shape=rectangle, label="Start", size=2];
A -> B [pos="0.5,0.5"];
```

In this example, the `size=2` makes the node twice as big as the default size, and `pos="0.5,0.5"` places the arrow in the middle of the node.

