`any` is a type that represents any value, and it is used when the type of a value is unknown or not important. Using `any` can make your code less type-safe, as TypeScript will not check the properties or methods of values of type `any`.

`never` is a type that represents a value that will never occur. It is used to represent functions that never return, or values that will never be assigned. For example, a function that throws an error and never returns can be given the type `never`.

